.TH "LAB" "1" "September 26, 2026" "Labrys" "User Commands"
.SH Name
lab \- inspect cluster resources and manage Kubeflow notebooks from a
terminal
.SH Synopsis
.IP
.EX
lab [\-\-cluster CLUSTER]
lab init
lab cluster add|remove [\-\-cluster\-id CLUSTER]
lab session start [\-\-request\-auth | \-\-token\-stdin] [\-\-json]
lab session close SESSION [\-\-json]
lab cluster list \-\-session SESSION [\-\-json]
lab cluster inspect CLUSTER \-\-session SESSION [\-\-json]
lab resources \-\-session SESSION \-\-cluster CLUSTER [\-\-namespace NAMESPACE ...] [\-\-json]
lab kubectl \-\-session SESSION \-\-cluster CLUSTER \-\- COMMAND [ARGS...]
lab notebook COMMAND \-\-cluster CLUSTER [OPTIONS]
lab auth \-\-request PATH
lab \-\-help
lab \-\-version
.EE
.PP
\f[CR]labrys\f[R] is an alias for \f[CR]lab\f[R].
Both commands accept the same arguments.
.SH Description
Labrys connects to a Kubernetes cluster using credentials stored in
1Password.
It can create a Kubeflow Notebook, open a container shell or VS Code
window, and start, stop or delete the Notebook.
.PP
Interactive lab and standalone Notebook commands each own one cluster
connection.
For repeated CLI access, \f[CR]lab session start\f[R] holds
authorization while other commands discover clusters, inspect resources
and run kubectl.
Each resource query and kubectl client gets its own connection to one
selected cluster.
.PP
Keep the owning lab process running while using its connections.
Closing local access does not stop or delete remote Notebooks.
Use \f[CR]stop\f[R] when you want to release a Notebook\(cqs compute
resources.
.PP
Run \f[CR]lab\f[R] without a subcommand to use the interactive menu.
Run \f[CR]lab notebook COMMAND\f[R] to perform one operation with
explicit arguments.
.SH Setup
Install Labrys and its manual as described in the source checkout\(cqs
README.
Setup needs the 1Password desktop app and a read\-only Service Account
with access to the vault holding lab\(cqs index, cluster credentials and
local\-data encryption key.
.PP
Run \f[CR]lab init\f[R], enter the Service Account Token in the hidden
prompt, and follow the instructions to create or select the encryption
key and connection index.
Choose an absolute directory for private cluster profiles.
Because the Service Account is read\-only, you save the proposed
documents in the 1Password app; lab reads them back to verify the
content.
Copy copies the full document but does not save it in 1Password.
.PP
Prepare \f[CR]CLUSTER.yaml\f[R] in that directory using your
cluster\(cqs creation rules.
The checkout contains \f[CR]profiles/template.yaml\f[R] and the
repository\-local \f[CR]lab\-profile\f[R] Agent Skill.
The template uses fictional values and must be adapted to your
environment.
Register the prepared profile, then open a session:
.IP
.EX
lab cluster add \-\-cluster\-id research\-example
lab \-\-cluster research\-example
.EE
.PP
Shell and editor access require \f[CR]kubectl\f[R] on \f[CR]PATH\f[R].
SSH connections also require \f[CR]ssh\f[R] and an existing SSH host
configuration.
Editor access requires \f[CR]code\f[R] on \f[CR]PATH\f[R]; the adapter
currently accepts VS Code 1.137.0 with Dev Containers 0.469.0.
Other versions are rejected by the compatibility check.
.SH Interactive use
The session menu offers Notebook actions, Cluster resources and
connection controls.
Choose an action or type a Notebook command without the
\f[CR]lab notebook\f[R] prefix, for example
\f[CR]list \-\-namespace research\f[R].
Forms ask for missing values and show the manifest before creation.
.PP
Within the menu, \f[CR]status\f[R] describes the lab connection;
\f[CR]notebook status\f[R] describes a Notebook.
\f[CR]disconnect\f[R] or \f[CR]exit\f[R] closes the session.
After a connection loss, \f[CR]reconnect\f[R] restores access using the
original authorization deadline.
Use another terminal for a second session or cluster.
.PP
Tab and Shift\-Tab move between controls, Enter advances or submits, and
Escape returns.
Tab accepts a grey field suggestion; typing replaces it.
Up and Down select history or suggestions.
F1 or Help explains the focused field.
.PP
After a new Notebook becomes Ready, you can save its settings as a
preset.
Presets exclude the instance name and are scoped to the cluster and
namespace.
Loading a preset fills the form; editing the form does not change the
saved preset.
The menu\(cqs \f[CR]save\-preset\f[R] action can also save a completed
creation by operation ID.
.SS Cluster resources
Choose \f[B]Cluster resources\f[R] or type \f[CR]resources\f[R] in the
session menu to browse node inventory and resource headroom.
The usual namespace form appears first; its answer is remembered as a
discovery hint, not a restriction on aggregation.
Login remains independent of namespace.
The overview summarizes the cluster; the node list supports search,
filters, sorting and pagination.
\f[CR]Enter\f[R] shows a node\(cqs allocatable resources, observed
reservations and taints.
This view uses the current connection and does not require a Notebook
creation profile.
For noninteractive use,
\f[CR]lab resources \-\-session SESSION \-\-cluster CLUSTER\f[R] prints
a table from the same resource reader.
Add \f[CR]\-\-json\f[R] for structured output.
.PP
Use \f[CR]/\f[R] to search, arrow keys to move, Left/Right or Page
Up/Down to change pages, \f[CR]f\f[R] to filter by headroom,
\f[CR]t\f[R] to filter advertised model labels, and \f[CR]s\f[R] to
sort.
\f[CR]g\f[R] selects an extended resource type, keeping different GPU
partitions and shared resource units separate.
\f[CR]c\f[R] clears filters, \f[CR]r\f[R] refreshes, \f[CR]?\f[R] shows
help, and Escape returns.
CPU and memory are shown alongside the selected resource.
.PP
The calculation subtracts effective requests of visible, assigned pods
from each node\(cqs allocatable resources.
It includes init containers, restartable sidecars, pod\-level budgets
and overhead.
These are reservations, not measured utilization.
Completed and unassigned pods are filtered by the API server before
pagination; assigned Pending and terminating pods remain counted.
Requests that cannot be reliably accounted for, including resizing pods
and DRA claims, are excluded from observed reservations and the result
is labelled as an upper bound.
Other readable reservations still contribute.
DRA device inventory is not included.
.PP
Lab first attempts cluster\-wide pod listing.
If access is denied, it discovers namespace names and queries each one
independently, adding names from this cluster\(cqs history, optional
creation profile and selected kubeconfig context.
Unreadable namespaces do not discard reservations found in readable
namespaces.
Partial observations show \f[CR]≤\f[R] upper bounds.
If no reservations can be read, the view still shows allocatable
capacity ceilings without claiming that devices are free.
Kubernetes can deny namespace discovery too; \f[CR]n\f[R] adds extra
namespace names and Lab remembers them for future visits.
.PP
The overview\(cqs remaining totals use Ready, uncordoned nodes.
Taints, namespace quotas, queued work and other placement rules may
further restrict what a job can obtain.
Refresh failures keep the previous timestamped snapshot with a warning.
.SH AI agents and automation
Use a Notebook prepared by the user by default.
Create, start, stop or delete a Notebook only when the user explicitly
asks.
Native kubectl keeps the cluster\(cqs existing permissions; lab does not
restrict its verbs or apply Notebook creation profiles to raw Kubernetes
requests.
.PP
Start one authorization process and keep it running:
.IP
.EX
lab session start \-\-request\-auth \-\-json
.EE
.PP
lab prints an authorization command to stderr and waits up to five
minutes.
Show that command to the user unchanged.
They run it in their own terminal, review the scope and enter their
Service Account Token in a hidden prompt.
The token goes directly to lab through a private socket; do not request
it in chat or tool output.
.PP
After successful authorization, stdout contains a \f[CR]Ready\f[R] JSON
result with \f[CR]data.session\f[R], \f[CR]data.expires_in\f[R] and
\f[CR]data.idle_timeout\f[R].
Keep the process alive; a command runner\(cqs short timeout would end
the authorization.
The session ID identifies a local, same\-user socket, not a token.
In the following examples, replace \f[CR]lab\-session\-example\f[R] with
the returned ID.
.PP
Discover registered clusters before selecting one:
.IP
.EX
lab cluster list \-\-session lab\-session\-example \-\-json
lab cluster inspect research\-example \-\-session lab\-session\-example \-\-json
.EE
.PP
Listing reads the 1Password connection index.
Inspection returns connection metadata and local creation rules, without
credentials.
A missing profile is reported but does not prevent kubectl access.
Profile namespaces describe creation rules, not the full set of
namespaces the Kubernetes credential can access.
.PP
Query resource availability without navigating the TUI:
.IP
.EX
lab resources \-\-session lab\-session\-example \-\-cluster research\-example \(rs
  \-\-namespace research \-\-json
.EE
.PP
Repeat \f[CR]\-\-namespace\f[R] to add discovery hints.
Lab also uses the selected kubeconfig context\(cqs explicit namespace
and optional profile namespaces; it still attempts cluster\-wide reads
and every namespace it can discover.
The reusable CLI session does not need the encrypted Notebook history or
its encryption key.
.PP
Read \f[CR]data.partial\f[R], \f[CR]data.scope\f[R] and
\f[CR]data.issues\f[R] before interpreting the numbers.
\f[CR]data.nodes\f[R] contains per\-node counts; \f[CR]data.summary\f[R]
contains totals.
Counts use the units in \f[CR]data.units\f[R]: CPU is an integer number
of millicores, memory is an integer number of bytes, and extended
resources are integer units.
GPU resource units can represent devices, partitions or shared slots.
.PP
\f[CR]observed_reserved: null\f[R] means no reservations were readable,
not zero usage.
\f[CR]remaining_upper_bound\f[R] is never a scheduling guarantee.
It is \f[CR]null\f[R] for nodes that are NotReady or cordoned; remaining
totals include Ready, uncordoned nodes only.
Namespace quotas, taints and workload placement can further restrict
access.
Individual node \f[CR]issue\f[R] values describe incomplete accounting.
A successful partial query has status \f[CR]partial\f[R] and exit code
0; authentication, transport and other failures use the usual error
result and nonzero exit code.
.PP
The command prints one JSON document, never prompts for input, and
closes its own connection without closing the reusable authorization.
Use a fresh session after upgrading Labrys to make newly added commands
available in the session owner.
.PP
Use native kubectl to find the user\(cqs Notebook and its Pod:
.IP
.EX
lab kubectl \-\-session lab\-session\-example \-\-cluster research\-example \-\- \(rs
  get notebooks.kubeflow.org \-n research \-o json
lab kubectl \-\-session lab\-session\-example \-\-cluster research\-example \-\- \(rs
  get pods \-n research \-l notebook\-name=example\-notebook \-o json
.EE
.PP
Use names from the results to choose the target.
The examples are fictional.
For a persistent shell, start an interactive command in a
terminal\-capable runner:
.IP
.EX
lab kubectl \-\-session lab\-session\-example \-\-cluster research\-example \-\- \(rs
  exec \-it example\-notebook\-0 \-n research \-c main \-\- /bin/sh
.EE
.PP
Keep that command running and send later input to the same terminal.
Its working directory and shell variables persist.
Open another client for concurrent work.
The same authorization can serve other indexed clusters; each client
establishes its own connection.
Standard input, stdout, stderr and kubectl\(cqs exit code pass through
unchanged, so pipes and native \f[CR]\-o json\f[R] work normally.
.PP
Before reporting completion, explicitly close your local access:
.IP
.EX
lab session close lab\-session\-example \-\-json
.EE
.PP
Closing authorization disconnects its clients and removes their
temporary credentials and routes.
It does not revoke the Service Account Token itself, stop or delete
Notebooks, or send remote process\-termination commands.
A later visit starts with a new authorization.
.PP
\f[B]Tip:\f[R] If an experiment should keep running after you
disconnect, consider using a tool such as \f[CR]tmux\f[R] inside the
container so you can detach and reconnect later.
Closing an ordinary exec terminal may interrupt its foreground work.
.PP
Authorization also ends at the index\(cqs absolute lifetime limit,
including laptop sleep, or after 15 minutes with no running request or
client.
A live shell, log follower or port\-forward counts as active even when
silent.
This idle rule does not try to distinguish an unused prompt from a quiet
experiment.
.PP
The existing \f[CR]lab notebook\f[R] commands remain available for
explicit Notebook workflows.
They require their own authorization and do not accept
\f[CR]\-\-session\f[R].
Use \f[CR]\-\-json \-\-request\-auth\f[R] for a standalone
machine\-readable operation.
Each request authorizes only that invocation; use \f[CR]\-\-yes\f[R] for
a requested mutation that would otherwise ask for confirmation.
Creation submits without a second confirmation.
Preserve its \f[CR]operation_id\f[R] after an interrupted response and
inspect the original operation before retrying.
.PP
\f[CR]lab\-credential\f[R] is the internal Kubernetes helper used by
kubectl and editor clients.
It uses an existing connection grant; it does not prompt for the
1Password token.
The user supplies that token through \f[CR]lab auth\f[R].
Reusable sessions are local to the same user account and assume trusted
installed code and local clients.
.SH Commands
.SS session start
Authorize reusable local access and stay in the foreground until closed
or expired.
The default is a hidden token prompt; \f[CR]\-\-request\-auth\f[R]
accepts a private handoff from another terminal, and
\f[CR]\-\-token\-stdin\f[R] accepts a trusted input pipe.
The session reads the connection index without requiring a cluster ID,
private creation profile or local\-history encryption key.
No token is saved on disk.
.SS session close SESSION
End the selected authorization and wait for its cluster connections to
be cleaned up.
\f[CR]Closed\f[R] confirms that lab\(cqs access routes and credential
grants are cleaned up; local kubectl processes may finish exiting
shortly afterward.
Cleanup failures are reported as errors rather than successful closure.
Remote Notebooks are left on the cluster.
.SS cluster list
With \f[CR]\-\-session SESSION\f[R], read the current index and return
\f[CR]clusters\f[R], a list of objects containing registered
\f[CR]id\f[R] values.
This does not connect to Kubernetes.
.SS cluster inspect CLUSTER
With \f[CR]\-\-session SESSION\f[R], read the registered connection and
return \f[CR]id\f[R], \f[CR]context\f[R], \f[CR]server\f[R],
\f[CR]transport\f[R] and \f[CR]profile\f[R].
The profile contains the local creation rules.
If it is unavailable, \f[CR]profile\f[R] is null and
\f[CR]profile_error\f[R] explains why.
Kubernetes credentials and 1Password item bodies are not returned.
.SS resources
Read a fresh node resource snapshot using
\f[CR]\-\-session SESSION \-\-cluster CLUSTER\f[R].
The default output is a text table.
\f[CR]\-\-json\f[R] emits the standard Labrys result envelope with
\f[CR]schema_version: 1\f[R], \f[CR]operation: \(dqresources\(dq\f[R], a
cluster target, and the following \f[CR]data\f[R] fields:
.PP
