.PP
Resource values are integers; JSON values are not rounded or formatted
with \f[CR]≤\f[R].
The \f[CR]remaining_upper_bound\f[R] name carries that meaning.
\f[CR]observed_reserved\f[R] is \f[CR]null\f[R] when no pod reservations
were readable.
Per\-node \f[CR]remaining_upper_bound\f[R] is \f[CR]null\f[R] when the
node is not Ready or is cordoned.
Omitted resource keys mean that the node did not advertise that
resource.
The report includes CPU, memory and advertised extended resources,
without raw Pod objects or credentials.
Reports still contain cluster metadata; do not use live reports as
public examples or test fixtures.
.PP
\f[CR]\-\-namespace NAME\f[R] adds a discovery hint and can be repeated.
It does not limit the query to those namespaces.
Existing permissions still determine which reads succeed; a missing
creation profile does not prevent the command from running.
The command does not prompt or accept a new token, and it leaves the
reusable authorization open.
It closes its own cluster connection, including when the caller
disconnects or authorization expires.
.SS kubectl \(en COMMAND [ARGS\&...]
With \f[CR]\-\-session SESSION \-\-cluster CLUSTER\f[R], run the
installed kubectl through a fresh connection using the existing
authorization.
The first \f[CR]\-\-\f[R] separates lab options from kubectl arguments.
A later \f[CR]\-\-\f[R], for example in \f[CR]kubectl exec\f[R], is
passed through to kubectl normally.
.PP
kubectl inherits the caller\(cqs environment, including cloud
authentication and proxy settings.
lab supplies \f[CR]KUBECONFIG\f[R] and its client grant, and passes all
arguments unchanged.
The Service Account Token received by lab is not added to the client
environment.
Explicit native connection options follow kubectl\(cqs normal behavior.
A connection using lab\(cqs credential grant must match that grant\(cqs
cluster identity.
If you explicitly select other credentials, their access is outside the
lab authorization; closing lab does not revoke them.
.PP
lab does not interpret or wrap kubectl\(cqs output.
A failed connection can be retried with a new invocation while the
authorization remains valid.
.SS init
Configure this device as described under Setup.
When selecting an existing index, lab checks its encryption key against
any local encrypted data.
.SS cluster add
Register a prepared \f[CR].yaml\f[R] profile.
Omit \f[CR]\-\-cluster\-id\f[R] to select an unregistered profile from
the configured directory.
Unavailable entries explain their error; Refresh rereads the directory
and index.
.PP
Choose an existing 1Password connection note, paste a kubeconfig, or
import a kubeconfig file.
For imports, select the context and route, review the connection and
save the proposed note in 1Password.
lab verifies the connection, then asks you to save and verify the
updated index.
Registration does not create a Notebook.
.SS cluster remove
Remove a registration from the index.
Omit \f[CR]\-\-cluster\-id\f[R] to select an entry.
Save the proposed index in 1Password and confirm its read\-back.
Removing a registration leaves the profile, connection note, local
history and remote Notebooks intact.
Existing lab sessions remain authorized.
.PP
Avoid concurrent index edits: a manual save and lab\(cqs read\-back are
separate operations.
Recheck repeats verification; Cancel does not undo a saved change.
.SS notebook list
List Notebooks and their observed state in one namespace.
.SS notebook create
Create a Notebook from explicit fields or a saved preset.
The standalone command prints a manifest preview and submits it without
a confirmation prompt.
The interactive form asks for confirmation.
.PP
The default result is \f[CR]Accepted\f[R], meaning Kubernetes accepted
the object.
Use \f[CR]\-\-wait\f[R] to wait for a Ready Pod.
A failed or expired wait leaves the Notebook on the cluster; inspect its
status before retrying.
.SS notebook status NAME
Show a Notebook\(cqs UID, requested stop state, observed lifecycle and
Pod names and states.
Use native kubectl to read its full spec, including configured
resources.
Use \f[CR]\-\-operation\-id\f[R] instead of name and namespace to
inspect a recorded creation.
Resource values describe requests, not measured usage.
.PP
\f[CR]Running\f[R] means an owned Pod is Ready.
A stop request remains \f[CR]Stopping\f[R] until the owned Pods are
gone, then becomes \f[CR]Stopped\f[R].
Other states include \f[CR]Starting\f[R], \f[CR]Error\f[R] and
\f[CR]Unknown\f[R].
A failed API query is reported as an error.
.SS notebook shell NAME
Open a shell in a Ready container.
\f[CR]\-\-pod\f[R] and \f[CR]\-\-container\f[R] resolve ambiguous
targets.
A controlling terminal is required.
The remote shell is owned by this lab invocation; start a terminal
multiplexer yourself if you need one.
.SS notebook open NAME
Request a VS Code window attached to a Ready container.
The lab process stays in the foreground to keep the editor connection
authorized.
Exiting lab leaves the window open but disconnected.
A new authorized session opens a new window.
.SS notebook start|stop|delete NAME
Start or stop the Notebook through the Kubeflow controller, or delete
the Notebook object.
These commands confirm the selected object before changing it.
\f[CR]\-\-yes\f[R] skips that confirmation for this invocation.
.PP
Start and stop return when the change is accepted, before the controller
finishes.
Use \f[CR]status\f[R] to observe progress.
Stopping releases the running Pod while retaining the Notebook
configuration.
Deleting also removes that configuration.
lab does not delete the files in the host directory mounted as Notebook
data; temporary container and \f[CR]emptyDir\f[R] data do not survive
Pod removal.
.SS notebook retry
Reconcile a recorded creation using \f[CR]\-\-operation\-id\f[R].
If the original object exists, verify its identity.
Submit again only after a confirmed absence and a check against the
current profile.
A changed profile or conflicting object blocks replay.
The command asks for confirmation unless \f[CR]\-\-yes\f[R] is supplied.
.SS notebook editor\-restart NAME
This operation is currently unavailable: no image/build profiles are
qualified, so it returns \f[CR]Unsupported\f[R] before remote execution.
\f[CR]\-\-yes\f[R] cannot bypass this check.
Ordinary \f[CR]open\f[R] is available with the editor versions listed
under Setup.
.SS auth \(enrequest PATH
Supply a token to one waiting \f[CR]\-\-request\-auth\f[R] invocation
from another terminal.
Run the exact command printed by the requester.
Review the displayed operation, then enter the token in the hidden
prompt.
This command accepts only \f[CR]\-\-request\f[R].
It does not create a reusable authorization service.
.SH Options
Common Notebook options can appear before or after \f[CR]notebook\f[R]
or its command.
Standalone Notebook commands require \f[CR]\-\-cluster\f[R] and
\f[CR]\-\-namespace\f[R].
Commands targeting an existing Notebook also require its positional
name.
An operation ID supplies name and namespace for \f[CR]status\f[R] and
\f[CR]retry\f[R]; omit both in that case.
Inside a session, forms can ask for missing values.
.PP
