.TE
.SH Examples
These examples use fictional cluster names, images and paths.
Replace them with values allowed by your private profile.
.PP
List Notebooks and inspect one:
.IP
.EX
lab notebook list \-\-cluster research\-example \-\-namespace research
lab notebook status example\-notebook \-\-cluster research\-example \(rs
  \-\-namespace research
.EE
.PP
Create a CPU Notebook and wait up to five minutes for readiness:
.IP
.EX
lab notebook create \-\-cluster research\-example \(rs
  \-\-namespace research \(rs
  \-\-name example\-notebook \-\-owner example.researcher \(rs
  \-\-image registry.example.invalid/research/base:v1 \(rs
  \-\-gpus 0 \-\-cpu 2 \-\-memory 4Gi \(rs
  \-\-storage\-source /shared/projects/example \(rs
  \-\-mount\-path /work \-\-workdir /work \(rs
  \-\-wait \-\-timeout 300
.EE
.PP
Inspect or retry a creation after a lost response, using the operation
ID reported by the original command:
.IP
.EX
lab notebook status \-\-cluster research\-example \(rs
  \-\-operation\-id OPERATION_UUID
lab notebook retry \-\-cluster research\-example \(rs
  \-\-operation\-id OPERATION_UUID
.EE
.SH Output and exit status
Lab command results use \f[CR]schema_version: 1\f[R] with
\f[CR]\-\-json\f[R].
Previews and diagnostics use stderr.
\f[CR]session start\f[R] prints its readiness result, then remains
running; normal closure is reported on stderr.
Cluster discovery uses JSON for machine output and formatted JSON for
human output.
The \f[CR]kubectl\f[R] command passes native output and exit codes
through without a lab JSON envelope.
Successful results contain \f[CR]operation\f[R], \f[CR]status\f[R],
\f[CR]target\f[R] and \f[CR]data\f[R].
Errors contain \f[CR]status: \(dqerror\(dq\f[R], \f[CR]error\f[R],
\f[CR]code\f[R], \f[CR]target\f[R] and \f[CR]data\f[R].
.PP
Creation results include an \f[CR]operation_id\f[R] and, when known, the
server object\(cqs \f[CR]uid\f[R].
Keep them after an interrupted request: failure to receive or save a
response does not mean the Notebook was not created.
.PP
